Chapter 7B research completed without scoring. The attempt directory is read-only, so the cumulative manifest could not be updated on disk; a recovery manifest is included below.

The evidence is mixed and attribution-sensitive. AMLO’s government maintained anti-corruption investigations and procurement sanctions, but independent assessments found politicized enforcement, weak transparency, attacks on critics, and limited accountability. Freedom House described selective anti-corruption enforcement and opacity around military and infrastructure matters. citeturn31view0 Human Rights Watch documented official harassment of journalists and obstruction concerns in the Ayotzinapa investigation. citeturn33view0turn33view2

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Mexico: Freedom in the World 2022 Country Report","publisher":"Freedom House","publication_date":"2022","url":"https://freedomhouse.org/country/mexico/freedom-world/2022","claim":"Freedom House reported that the López Obrador administration made anti-corruption a central theme, but critics viewed enforcement as politicized and ineffective, with investigations involving allies and favored institutions described as rare or perfunctory.","locator":"Corruption and transparency sections, lines 149-156.","provisional_id":"E-AMLO22-7B-01","canonical_fact_key":"https://freedomhouse.org/country/mexico/freedom-world/2022|lines 149-156|politicized enforcement and transparency limits","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["7B"],"methodology_ids":["7B.1","7B.2","7B.6","7B.7","7B.10"],"source_type":"comparative governance assessment","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent country report with explicit 2022 coverage and separate corruption/transparency findings.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly assesses Mexico during the target period.","ruler_attribution":"The report evaluates the administration and explicitly names López Obrador; personal responsibility is strongest for public leadership and executive enforcement priorities, weaker for every individual prosecution.","contrary_evidence":["The report acknowledges that some federal freedom-of-information laws strengthened transparency and that officials pursued cases against former-government figures."],"lenses":["7B.1","7B.2","7B.6","7B.7","7B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"En 2022, la SFP impone multas por más de mil 300 mdp a licitantes, proveedores y contratistas","publisher":"Secretaría de la Función Pública, Mexico","publication_date":"2023-01-30","url":"https://www.gob.mx/buengobierno/prensa/en-2022-la-sfp-impone-multas-por-mas-de-mil-300-mdp-a-licitantes-proveedores-y-contratistas","claim":"Mexico’s federal public-function authority reported 211 sanctions against 197 contractors and suppliers in 2022, including 178 disqualifications and fines totaling 1.325 billion pesos for false information, contract failures, and simulated compliance.","locator":"Official communiqué, lines 53-67.","provisional_id":"E-AMLO22-7B-02","canonical_fact_key":"https://www.gob.mx/buengobierno/prensa/en-2022-la-sfp-impone-multas-por-mas-de-mil-300-mdp-a-licitantes-proveedores-y-contratistas|lines 53-67|2022 procurement sanctions","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["7B"],"methodology_ids":["7B.3","7B.6","7B.9"],"source_type":"official procurement-enforcement record","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary government enforcement report with dates, sanction counts, legal bases, and stated reasons.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Reports actions covering January-December 2022.","ruler_attribution":"The sanctions show institutional enforcement under AMLO but do not establish his personal involvement in individual cases.","contrary_evidence":["Government self-reporting may emphasize enforcement successes and does not establish whether politically connected actors were treated equally."],"lenses":["7B.3","7B.6","7B.9"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Presidente instruye transparencia total en caso Segalmex; Gobierno de México recupera montos y mantiene denuncias","publisher":"Presidencia de la República, Mexico","publication_date":"unknown","url":"https://amlo.presidente.gob.mx/presidente-instruye-transparencia-total-en-caso-segalmex-gobierno-de-mexico-recupera-montos-y-mantiene-denuncias/","claim":"The government reported that audits of Segalmex identified 9.5 billion pesos in observed possible damage, that 955 million pesos were recovered from illicitly invested funds, and that unresolved amounts were referred for criminal and administrative proceedings.","locator":"Government briefing, lines 39-77.","provisional_id":"E-AMLO22-7B-03","canonical_fact_key":"https://amlo.presidente.gob.mx/presidente-instruye-transparencia-total-en-caso-segalmex-gobierno-de-mexico-recupera-montos-y-mantiene-denuncias/|lines 39-77|Segalmex audits, recovery, and referrals","disposition":"context","chapter_ids":["7B"],"methodology_ids":["7B.2","7B.6","7B.7","7B.9"],"source_type":"official executive briefing","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary government account of investigations and referrals, but it is also an interested official narrative.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Discusses the 2019-2022 crisis and subsequent investigative response; not wholly contemporaneous with 2022.","ruler_attribution":"AMLO personally instructed transparency and investigative coordination according to the briefing; the underlying alleged misconduct is attributed to Segalmex officials, not automatically to the ruler.","contrary_evidence":["The same briefing stresses that audit observations are preliminary and not equivalent to legally established damage or guilt."],"lenses":["7B.2","7B.6","7B.7","7B.9"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"21.02.22 Versión estenográfica de la conferencia de prensa matutina","publisher":"Presidencia de la República, Mexico","publication_date":"2022-02-21","url":"https://amlo.presidente.gob.mx/21-02-22-version-estenografica-de-la-conferencia-de-prensa-matutina-del-presidente-andres-manuel-lopez-obrador/","claim":"During a February 2022 press conference, AMLO acknowledged Segalmex complaints and investigations, said no one should be judged without proof, and promised that corruption would be investigated and punished if established; he also disparaged autonomous anti-corruption and transparency institutions as ineffective or deceptive.","locator":"Transcript, lines 456-489.","provisional_id":"E-AMLO22-7B-04","canonical_fact_key":"https://amlo.presidente.gob.mx/21-02-22-version-estenografica-de-la-conferencia-de-prensa-matutina-del-presidente-andres-manuel-lopez-obrador/|lines 456-489|Segalmex response and attacks on autonomous oversight","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["7B"],"methodology_ids":["7B.1","7B.2","7B.6","7B.7","7B.10"],"source_type":"official presidential transcript","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous primary record of the ruler’s own public statements.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly dated within the target year.","ruler_attribution":"Direct personal attribution for the statements; effects on institutions require independent corroboration.","contrary_evidence":["His insistence on proof and investigation can support procedural restraint, while his rhetoric against oversight institutions can support an adverse interpretation."],"lenses":["7B.1","7B.2","7B.6","7B.7","7B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Ejecutivo federal aclara relación contractual entre Pemex y Baker Hughes","publisher":"Presidencia de la República, Mexico","publication_date":"2022-02-09","url":"https://www.gob.mx/amlo/prensa/ejecutivo-federal-aclara-relacion-contractual-entre-pemex-y-baker-hughes-la-honestidad-es-escudo-que-proteg","claim":"AMLO publicly denied that Baker Hughes received a direct 89-million-dollar assignment and instructed Pemex’s director to explain the company’s contracts, while the official statement asserted that there were no favored companies.","locator":"Official communiqué, lines 50-64.","provisional_id":"E-AMLO22-7B-05","canonical_fact_key":"https://www.gob.mx/amlo/prensa/ejecutivo-federal-aclara-relacion-contractual-entre-pemex-y-baker-hughes-la-honestidad-es-escudo-que-proteg|lines 50-64|Baker Hughes denial and official explanation","disposition":"context","chapter_ids":["7B"],"methodology_ids":["7B.1","7B.2","7B.3","7B.4","7B.8"],"source_type":"official presidential response","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary record of the government’s stated position, but it is not an independent finding that resolves the conflict-of-interest controversy.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Directly dated February 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable as AMLO’s public response and instruction to Pemex; the underlying family/company relationship remains a separate factual question.","contrary_evidence":["Independent reporting and a federal investigation were reported at the time; the official denial should not be treated as dispositive."],"lenses":["7B.1","7B.2","7B.3","7B.4","7B.8"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Mexico: Address Persistent Violence Against Journalists","publisher":"Human Rights Watch","publication_date":"2022-05-03","url":"https://www.hrw.org/news/2022/05/03/mexico-address-persistent-violence-against-journalists","claim":"Human Rights Watch reported eight journalist killings in Mexico by May 2022, near-total impunity, official harassment of critical journalists, and a protection mechanism that was underfunded and understaffed.","locator":"Findings and recommendations, lines 606-625.","provisional_id":"E-AMLO22-7B-06","canonical_fact_key":"https://www.hrw.org/news/2022/05/03/mexico-address-persistent-violence-against-journalists|lines 606-625|journalist violence, official harassment, and impunity","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["7B"],"methodology_ids":["7B.1","7B.2","7B.6","7B.7","7B.10"],"source_type":"human-rights investigation","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent rights organization cites journalist-tracking and government-protection data and provides specific period findings.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly covers the target year.","ruler_attribution":"The report directly attributes public harassment to AMLO and assigns broader protection failures to federal authorities; criminal killings themselves cannot be attributed personally without case-specific evidence.","contrary_evidence":["The federal protection mechanism existed and covered hundreds of journalists and defenders, and the government proposed reforms."],"lenses":["7B.1","7B.2","7B.6","7B.7","7B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Obstrucción oficial sigue impidiendo la justicia en el caso Ayotzinapa","publisher":"Human Rights Watch","publication_date":"2022-10-19","url":"https://www.hrw.org/es/news/2022/10/19/obstruccion-oficial-sigue-impidiendo-la-justicia-en-el-caso-ayotzinapa","claim":"Human Rights Watch reported that although the AMLO administration created a special investigation and obtained arrest warrants in 2022, subsequent events raised concerns that the attorney general obstructed the investigation, including by failing to execute orders and interfering with the special prosecutor.","locator":"Spanish-language report, lines 364-374.","provisional_id":"E-AMLO22-7B-07","canonical_fact_key":"https://www.hrw.org/es/news/2022/10/19/obstruccion-oficial-sigue-impidiendo-la-justicia-en-el-caso-ayotzinapa|lines 364-374|Ayotzinapa investigative progress and alleged obstruction","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["7B"],"methodology_ids":["7B.2","7B.5","7B.6","7B.7","7B.8"],"source_type":"human-rights investigation","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Uses the Inter-American expert group’s findings and identifies both favorable institutional steps and adverse investigative developments.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly addresses developments in 2022.","ruler_attribution":"AMLO’s creation of the truth-seeking framework and instruction to cooperate are attributable; alleged prosecutorial obstruction is attributed to the attorney general and other authorities, not automatically to AMLO personally.","contrary_evidence":["The administration created a special unit, appointed a respected rights defender, and secured 83 arrest warrants in August 2022."],"lenses":["7B.2","7B.5","7B.6","7B.7","7B.8"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Mexico: Freedom on the Net 2022 Country Report","publisher":"Freedom House","publication_date":"2022","url":"https://freedomhouse.org/country/mexico/freedom-net/2022","claim":"Freedom House reported continued violence against online reporters, evidence of spyware against dissenting voices, politicized content-removal and narrative-amplification efforts, and government policies that threatened existing information-access safeguards.","locator":"Country overview and political-content sections, lines 84, 260-261, and 325.","provisional_id":"E-AMLO22-7B-08","canonical_fact_key":"https://freedomhouse.org/country/mexico/freedom-net/2022|lines 84,260-261,325|online repression, spyware, and narrative manipulation","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["7B"],"methodology_ids":["7B.1","7B.2","7B.6","7B.7","7B.9","7B.10"],"source_type":"digital-rights assessment","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent country assessment with source-linked allegations, though some findings concern networks and policies rather than direct presidential orders.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"2022 country report with some historical evidence used to explain the target-year environment.","ruler_attribution":"The report attributes policies and safeguards to the administration; individual spyware use or online-network activity requires separate case-specific attribution.","contrary_evidence":["The report also notes constitutional guarantees for speech, press, privacy, and access to information."],"lenses":["7B.1","7B.2","7B.6","7B.7","7B.9","7B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"2022 begins with bloodbath for journalists in Mexico","publisher":"Reporters Without Borders","publication_date":"2022-01-21","url":"https://rsf.org/en/2022-begins-bloodbath-journalists-mexico-0","claim":"RSF reported two journalist murders in January 2022, failures to provide requested protection, and called on federal and state authorities to investigate and reinforce journalist-protection mechanisms.","locator":"Report, lines 320-334.","provisional_id":"E-AMLO22-7B-09","canonical_fact_key":"https://rsf.org/en/2022-begins-bloodbath-journalists-mexico-0|lines 320-334|2022 journalist killings and protection failures","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["7B"],"methodology_ids":["7B.1","7B.6","7B.7","7B.10"],"source_type":"press-freedom monitoring","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Specialist press-freedom organization reports specific cases, dates, protection requests, and official responsibilities.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly dated January 2022.","ruler_attribution":"The cases implicate federal and state protection systems; they do not establish personal AMLO direction of the killings or local failures.","contrary_evidence":["The report records existing federal and state protection mechanisms and calls for strengthening rather than abolition."],"lenses":["7B.1","7B.6","7B.7","7B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Proceso para ejercer el Derecho de Acceso a la Información","publisher":"Instituto Nacional de Transparencia, Acceso a la Información y Protección de Datos Personales (INAI)","publication_date":"unknown","url":"https://home.inai.org.mx/?page_id=1643","claim":"INAI’s process allowed requesters to challenge information denials through review appeals filed electronically, in writing, or through the transparency unit, including reasonable accommodations for disabled or Indigenous-language users.","locator":"INAI guidance, lines 241-257.","provisional_id":"E-AMLO22-7B-10","canonical_fact_key":"https://home.inai.org.mx/?page_id=1643|lines 241-257|information-access appeal mechanism","disposition":"context","chapter_ids":["7B"],"methodology_ids":["7B.6","7B.7","7B.9"],"source_type":"independent transparency-institution guidance","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary institutional description of the formal information-access remedy.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Institutional mechanism existed during the target period, but the page lacks a clear publication date.","ruler_attribution":"Shows an inherited accountability mechanism available during AMLO’s rule; it does not establish whether the executive tolerated or obstructed individual appeals.","contrary_evidence":["Freedom House reported noncompliance and opacity in some controversial areas despite the formal mechanism."],"lenses":["7B.6","7B.7","7B.9"]}

