Wave accounting:

- Documents discovered: approximately 30.
- Documents opened: 17 pages, reports, transcripts, and institutional records.
- Accepted: 10 new source-claim units.
- Reused: prior transparency, political-freedom, procurement, and investigative records where genuinely applicable.
- Rejected or discovery-only: unverified snippets, allegations lacking an opened primary or high-quality underlying source, and sources outside the target period.
- Access-blocked: 4 pages or PDFs, including some ASF and Animal Político records.
- Distinct organizations: Freedom House, Human Rights Watch, RSF, Transparency/INAI institutions, Mexico’s SFP and Presidency, and ASF discovery records.
- Source types: comparative assessments, human-rights investigations, press-freedom monitoring, official transcripts, procurement-enforcement records, and transparency-institution guidance.

Lens coverage is substantive for 7B.1, 7B.2, 7B.6, 7B.7, and 7B.10; partial for 7B.3, 7B.4, 7B.5, 7B.8, and 7B.9. The exact residual gaps are:

- no conclusive finding that AMLO or his family personally profited from office;
- incomplete evidence on appointment competence and nepotism;
- limited direct evidence on whether oversight institutions were selectively neutralized in 2022;
- unresolved conflict-of-interest allegations concerning Baker Hughes;
- limited evidence on campaign finance, asset declarations, pardons, and foreign-policy integrity;
- uncertain personal responsibility for local corruption, journalist killings, and prosecutorial decisions.

A further wave would have material value only if narrowly focused on official Baker Hughes/FGR/SFP records, ASF Segalmex and procurement audits, INAI resolutions, asset and conflict-of-interest disclosures, and appointment records. Broad corruption or press-freedom searching would mostly duplicate the current evidence.
